+# Voices of the Neighborhood: Grade 7 Reading Workshop
+
+## Program snapshot
+
+This after-school reading workshop runs for four weeks and serves Grade 7 only: 18 learners in exactly three stable groups of six. It meets Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday for 45 minutes, producing 12 sessions. The theme, “Voices of the Neighborhood,” asks learners to examine how genre, point of view, evidence, and language shape accounts of a shared place. Every task is completed during the workshop; there is no homework.
+
+Instruction uses all six texts in the provided packet: “Block by Block,” “The Lot That Became a Garden,” “Bus Stop at Dawn,” “Library Steps,” “When the Creek Rose,” and “Safe Crossings.” The only other materials used are the listed notebooks, pencils, sticky notes, highlighters, chart paper, markers, index cards, projector, and document camera. The sequence moves from supported close reading to comparison, discussion, and an independent cross-text response.
+
+## Program goals and success indicators
+
+| Goal | Measurable program goal | Numerical success indicator |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| G1 | Learners will determine a central idea and trace how at least two details develop it in literary and informational neighborhood texts. | By Session 12, at least 80% of learners will earn 3/4 or better on the central-idea portion of the common response rubric. |
+| G2 | Learners will select relevant textual evidence and explain how that evidence supports an inference or comparison rather than merely copying it. | By Session 12, at least 80% of learners will earn 3/4 or better on both evidence selection and reasoning in the parallel post-assessment. |
+| G3 | Learners will build on peers’ ideas in text-based discussion and synthesize more than one perspective in speaking or writing. | By Week 4, every group will show 5/6 members using both a cited detail and a peer-connection move during the final discussion. |
+
+## Grouping plan
+
+The 18 learners remain in exactly three stable groups of six so that discussion routines become predictable and evidence can be compared week to week. The teacher uses the Session 1 pre-assessment only to plan scaffolds within each group, not to reassign membership. Six roles rotate each session: facilitator, passage reader, evidence finder, connector, recorder, and reporter. A posted role card states one observable duty for each role. During independent assessment work, learners stay at group tables but complete and submit their own responses.
+
+Whole-group modeling is brief. Most practice follows an individual-think, partner-rehearse, group-synthesize pattern, so every learner first develops an idea and then receives a supported opportunity to speak. The teacher listens to one focal group per session while still collecting the named check from everyone.
+
+## Accessibility approach
+
+For multilingual learners, the plan repeatedly previews a small set of essential words with a visual or familiar example, preserves access to the complete grade-level text, allows oral rehearsal before writing, and supplies sentence frames that express the target reasoning rather than simplifying it. Learners may annotate a key word in another language and then connect it to the English phrase used in discussion.
+
+For learners with dyslexia, printed passages are uncluttered and chunked with generous spacing; the teacher or a peer reads directions and selected passages aloud while learners track the print. Learners receive extra processing time inside the same 45-minute agenda, may dictate an idea to a partner before recording it, and are assessed on the reading target rather than spelling. Color is never the only cue: every highlight code also has a letter or symbol.
+
+## Weekly evidence checkpoints
+
+| Week | Evidence reviewed | Instructional response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Week 1 | Compare each learner’s pre-assessment with the Session 3 two-text exit card, noting central-idea accuracy and whether cited details are relevant. | Begin Week 2 with one additional teacher model if fewer than 80% cite a relevant detail; otherwise move the model into group practice and confer with the remaining learners. |
+| Week 2 | Score the Session 6 comparison paragraph for claim, one detail from each text, and an explanation connecting both details to the claim. | Use the first ten minutes of Session 7 for a color-and-symbol evidence sort when a learner lacks either text; use a reasoning-frame conference when evidence is present but unexplained. |
+| Week 3 | Combine the Session 8 argument slip and Session 9 discussion tracker to identify who can distinguish a reason from evidence and connect to a peer. | Assign the next rotating role strategically for practice, then give a brief oral rehearsal prompt to learners missing a peer connection or an evidence explanation. |
+| Week 4 | Compare the Session 12 parallel post-assessment with the Session 1 baseline and review the final discussion tracker against all three success indicators. | Record one specific next-step skill for each learner and give the classroom teacher the group trend: central idea, evidence reasoning, or discussion synthesis needing continued practice. |
+
+## Assessment plan
+
+The Session 1 pre-assessment and Session 12 parallel post-assessment each include a fresh short packet excerpt, one central-idea item, one request for two supporting details, and a six-sentence evidence-and-reasoning response. Both are completed independently in 18 minutes and scored with the same four-point dimensions: central idea, evidence relevance, reasoning, and clarity. “Parallel” means the genre mix, directions, length, demand, timing, and rubric stay the same while the passage itself changes. The teacher records dimension scores rather than only a total, making growth and the next instructional response visible.
+
+Daily formative checks are brief products or observation records named in the sessions below. The teacher sorts each as secure, developing, or not yet, adds one dated note to the roster, and uses that evidence at the weekly checkpoint. Accessibility supports remain available on both assessments: oral reading of directions, a tracking guide made from an index card, and oral rehearsal of directions. The teacher does not read the assessed passage or formulate the learner’s answer.
+
+## Week 1, Session 1 — Tuesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Given an unfamiliar packet excerpt, learners will state a central idea, select two relevant details, and explain one connection independently for the pre-assessment baseline.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Welcome learners, introduce the neighborhood-voices theme, and explain that today’s first response establishes a starting point rather than a grade. |
+| 18 | Learners independently complete the pre-assessment while the teacher records completion conditions without coaching answers. |
+| 7 | Introduce the six rotating group roles and rehearse each duty with a neutral, non-text example. |
+| 10 | Project the opening of “Block by Block”; model noticing who is speaking, where the account occurs, and one repeated idea. |
+| 5 | Learners complete the baseline reflection card and name one reading move they want to strengthen. |
+
+**Materials:** Printed packet, notebooks, pencils, index cards, projector.
+
+**Formative check:** “Baseline reflection card”—each learner names one strategy used and one challenge; the teacher pairs it with the scored pre-assessment dimensions.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Preview speaker, transcript, and central idea with a projected example; allow quiet partner rehearsal of the reflection while keeping the pre-assessment response independent.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Read all directions aloud, provide an index-card tracking guide, and permit the learner to orally restate directions before independently reading and responding.
+
+## Week 1, Session 2 — Wednesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will annotate “Block by Block” to identify the speaker’s perspective and label three details that reveal how the market street changed.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Revisit the theme and preview perspective, change, and detail with a projected before-and-after example. |
+| 8 | Teacher models a think-aloud on one paragraph, marking P for perspective and D for a supporting detail. |
+| 12 | Learners read and annotate individually, then orally rehearse one annotation with a partner. |
+| 15 | Stable groups compare details, order three changes on sticky notes, and explain which words reveal the speaker’s attitude. |
+| 5 | Each learner submits a perspective-and-detail index card. |
+
+**Materials:** “Block by Block” in the printed packet, highlighters, sticky notes, index cards, pencils, document camera.
+
+**Formative check:** “Perspective-and-detail card”—one sentence identifies the perspective and one quoted or paraphrased detail is labeled with an explanation.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Provide the frame “The speaker views ___ as ___; the phrase ___ shows this because ___” and allow rehearsal before the card is written.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Chunk the transcript into short numbered sections, read the modeled paragraph aloud, and pair the P/D symbols with written labels rather than color alone.
+
+## Week 1, Session 3 — Thursday
+
+**Measurable objective:** After reading “Bus Stop at Dawn,” learners will write a central idea and explain how one image from the poem and one detail from “Block by Block” develop related neighborhood ideas.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 6 | Read “Bus Stop at Dawn” aloud twice as learners track print and jot one image they can picture or hear. |
+| 9 | Model moving from a repeated image to a central idea without reducing the idea to a one-word topic. |
+| 10 | Learners annotate two images and draft a central-idea statement independently. |
+| 15 | Groups place poem images beside details from “Block by Block” on chart paper and discuss one connection and one difference. |
+| 5 | Learners complete the two-text exit card used for the Week 1 checkpoint. |
+
+**Materials:** “Bus Stop at Dawn” and “Block by Block” in the printed packet, notebooks, pencils, highlighters, chart paper, markers.
+
+**Formative check:** “Two-text exit card”—each learner states the poem’s central idea and cites one relevant detail from each text with a one-sentence connection.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Preview image, sound, and central idea with concrete examples; offer “Both texts suggest ___, but the poem shows it through ___” for oral rehearsal.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Supply a spacious poem copy from the packet, pair the teacher read-aloud with finger tracking, and accept a dictated rehearsal before independent writing.
+
+## Week 2, Session 4 — Tuesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will identify the problem, response, and outcome in “The Lot That Became a Garden” and cite one detail supporting each part with 3/3 accuracy.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Review Week 1 evidence and state the day’s problem-response-outcome reading target. |
+| 8 | Project the feature’s opening and model labeling P, R, and O in the margin. |
+| 12 | Learners read assigned sections independently and mark a candidate detail for each label. |
+| 15 | Groups assemble a three-part chart, challenge weak evidence, and replace it with more relevant details. |
+| 5 | Learners complete an individual P-R-O evidence strip on an index card. |
+
+**Materials:** “The Lot That Became a Garden” in the printed packet, projector, highlighters, chart paper, markers, index cards.
+
+**Formative check:** “P-R-O evidence strip”—three labeled entries are checked for correct sequence and direct relevance to problem, response, and outcome.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Preview convert, vacant, resident, and outcome using the feature’s context; provide “Residents responded to ___ by ___, which led to ___.”
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Read the opening aloud, provide P/R/O word-and-letter labels, and let learners place sticky-note details before copying the selected evidence.
+
+## Week 2, Session 5 — Wednesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Given four candidate quotations from “The Lot That Became a Garden,” learners will rank them by relevance and justify the strongest evidence in two complete reasoning sentences.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Contrast “related to the topic” with “supports this exact claim” through a projected non-text example. |
+| 10 | Teacher models testing two quotations against a claim and underlining the words that make one stronger. |
+| 10 | Pairs rank four packet excerpts and record a reason for first and last place. |
+| 15 | Groups compare rankings, reach consensus, and compose an evidence-plus-reasoning explanation on chart paper. |
+| 5 | Learners independently submit a strongest-evidence slip. |
+
+**Materials:** Printed packet, pencils, highlighters, sticky notes, chart paper, markers, index cards, document camera.
+
+**Formative check:** “Strongest-evidence slip”—the learner selects one quotation and completes two sentences explaining precisely how its language supports the stated claim.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Post the reasoning connectors because, which shows, and therefore; permit partner rehearsal using the full frame before the independent slip.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Present candidate quotations as separately spaced numbered blocks, read each once aloud, and allow physical ranking with numbered sticky notes before writing.
+
+## Week 2, Session 6 — Thursday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will compose one comparison paragraph with a clear claim, one relevant detail from each of two texts, and reasoning that connects both details to the claim.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Revisit the strongest-evidence criteria and read the four-part paragraph success list aloud. |
+| 8 | Model a comparison claim linking “Block by Block” and “The Lot That Became a Garden.” |
+| 10 | Learners choose and label one detail from each text, then orally rehearse the relationship with a partner. |
+| 17 | Learners independently draft and check a paragraph for claim, two details, and reasoning while the teacher confers. |
+| 5 | Learners underline the claim, box evidence, bracket reasoning, and submit the paragraph for the Week 2 checkpoint. |
+
+**Materials:** “Block by Block” and “The Lot That Became a Garden” in the printed packet, notebooks, pencils, highlighters, document camera.
+
+**Formative check:** “Four-part comparison paragraph”—the teacher scores presence and quality of the claim, two-text evidence, and connecting reasoning on the checkpoint rubric.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Offer comparison stems such as “Both accounts show ___; while ___, both details reveal ___” and schedule oral rehearsal before drafting.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Keep the four-part checklist visible in words and symbols, allow speech-to-partner planning, and evaluate ideas and evidence separately from spelling.
+
+## Week 3, Session 7 — Tuesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will explain how first-person point of view shapes two details in “When the Creek Rose,” earning at least 2/3 points on an explanation check.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 10 | Respond to Week 2 evidence with either an evidence sort or a short reasoning-frame conference, then state the new target. |
+| 7 | Preview first person, narrator, and limitation; model changing one first-person sentence to an outside account. |
+| 10 | Learners read a packet section and mark N for what the narrator notices and L for a limit in what can be known. |
+| 13 | Groups discuss how two details feel different because the narrator experienced the flood and build one shared explanation. |
+| 5 | Learners submit a point-of-view cause/effect card. |
+
+**Materials:** “When the Creek Rose” in the printed packet, notebooks, pencils, sticky notes, index cards, projector.
+
+**Formative check:** “Point-of-view cause/effect card”—one detail and one limitation are linked to the narrator’s first-person position and scored on a three-point check.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Preview narrator, notices, and limitation with a familiar scenario; use “Because the narrator is inside the event, the reader learns ___ but not ___.”
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Read the selected section aloud while learners track it, use N/L letter-and-word cues, and let learners rehearse the cause/effect sentence orally.
+
+## Week 3, Session 8 — Wednesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will distinguish claim, reason, and evidence in “Safe Crossings” and justify which speaker provides the strongest-supported position with two cited details.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Preview public comment, claim, reason, and evidence and connect each to a simple school-based example. |
+| 9 | Model labeling C, R, and E in one resident’s comment and note that speakers may share a claim for different reasons. |
+| 12 | Learners annotate two additional comments independently, then compare labels with a partner. |
+| 14 | Groups rank the speakers by strength of support and defend the top choice with two details. |
+| 5 | Learners complete an individual argument-evidence slip. |
+
+**Materials:** “Safe Crossings” in the printed packet, pencils, highlighters, sticky notes, chart paper, markers, index cards.
+
+**Formative check:** “Argument-evidence slip”—each learner identifies one speaker’s claim, one reason, and one evidence detail, then explains whether the evidence is sufficient.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Post a claim/reason/evidence mini-glossary with examples and provide “The strongest-supported speaker is ___ because the details ___ and ___.”
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Separate each speaker’s excerpt with a bold numbered heading, read comments aloud by speaker, and pair C/R/E letters with their full words.
+
+## Week 3, Session 9 — Thursday
+
+**Measurable objective:** During a six-person discussion, every learner will cite one detail from “Library Steps” and use one move that accurately connects to a peer’s idea.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 6 | View “Library Steps” and silently choose one image-caption pair that communicates belonging or access. |
+| 8 | Model three connection moves: add supporting evidence, complicate an idea, or connect two peers’ observations. |
+| 8 | Learners prepare a claim, a cited caption detail, and a preferred connection stem in notebooks. |
+| 18 | Stable groups conduct a structured discussion while the teacher and group recorder track evidence and peer connections. |
+| 5 | Learners submit a synthesis card naming how their thinking changed or became more precise. |
+
+**Materials:** “Library Steps” in the printed packet, projector, notebooks, pencils, index cards, chart paper, markers.
+
+**Formative check:** “Evidence-and-connection discussion tracker”—the recorder marks one cited detail and one accurate peer-connection move for each named learner.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Allow advance selection and rehearsal of “I can add ___,” “I see it differently because ___,” or “Both ideas connect through ___.”
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Project one image-caption pair at a time, read captions aloud, and provide the three discussion moves on a spacious index card for quick reference.
+
+## Week 4, Session 10 — Tuesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will develop a defensible claim about how two packet texts portray community action and organize two relevant details plus reasoning in a response plan.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 10 | Respond to Week 3 evidence with targeted oral rehearsal of either reason/evidence distinctions or peer-connection moves. |
+| 7 | Model narrowing a broad topic into a claim that can be supported by two different texts. |
+| 10 | Learners select two texts, draft a claim, and locate one detail from each using notebook records and the packet. |
+| 13 | Partners test each plan for relevance and reasoning; learners revise the claim or replace weak evidence. |
+| 5 | Learners submit a two-text claim map. |
+
+**Materials:** All six texts in the printed packet, notebooks, pencils, highlighters, sticky notes, index cards, document camera.
+
+**Formative check:** “Two-text claim map”—the teacher checks that the claim is debatable, both details are relevant, and each planned reason answers how the evidence supports the claim.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Offer a choice of claim frames using portray, emphasize, and contrast; allow learners to explain the map orally before revising its English wording.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Use a four-box claim/detail/detail/reasoning organizer drawn in the notebook, read the success questions aloud, and allow added planning time within partner review.
+
+## Week 4, Session 11 — Wednesday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will draft and revise a cross-text response that meets at least four of five criteria: claim, two-text evidence, reasoning, organization, and synthesis.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 5 | Read the five criteria and inspect a projected anonymous model for one strength and one revision need. |
+| 8 | Teacher models revising a copied detail into evidence followed by explicit reasoning. |
+| 17 | Learners draft independently from their Session 10 claim maps while the teacher conducts brief evidence conferences. |
+| 10 | Partners use two sticky notes to identify one met criterion and ask one text-based revision question. |
+| 5 | Learners make one visible revision and submit a revision note naming the criterion improved. |
+
+**Materials:** Printed packet, notebooks, pencils, sticky notes, highlighters, projector, document camera.
+
+**Formative check:** “Five-criteria revision note”—the draft is checked for all five criteria, and the learner points to a specific revision that improves one previously weak criterion.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Supply transition choices for adding evidence, contrasting perspectives, and explaining significance; encourage oral rehearsal of the revised sentence before writing.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Display one criterion per line with a matching symbol, separate drafting from spelling review, and allow a partner to read the learner’s sentence back verbatim.
+
+## Week 4, Session 12 — Thursday
+
+**Measurable objective:** Learners will independently complete the parallel post-assessment and then identify one rubric dimension that improved from the pre-assessment with specific evidence.
+
+**Timed agenda (45 minutes):**
+
+| Minutes | Activity |
+| --- | --- |
+| 4 | Reestablish independent assessment conditions and read the unchanged directions and four rubric dimensions aloud. |
+| 18 | Learners complete the parallel post-assessment using a fresh excerpt and the same demands and timing as the pre-assessment. |
+| 8 | Teacher returns dimension-only baseline information; learners compare it with a self-check of today’s response. |
+| 10 | Stable groups hold a closing round in which each learner names one reading move and one packet text that helped develop it. |
+| 5 | Learners submit an individual growth-and-next-step card for the Week 4 checkpoint. |
+
+**Materials:** Printed packet, notebooks, pencils, index cards, projector.
+
+**Formative check:** “Growth-and-next-step card”—each learner cites a pre/post rubric dimension, points to evidence of growth, and names one precise next reading step.
+
+**Multilingual learner support:** Read directions aloud and clarify task verbs without paraphrasing the assessed passage; provide reflection frames only after the independent post-assessment is submitted.
+
+**Dyslexia support:** Provide the same spaced layout and index-card tracking guide used at baseline, allow oral restatement of directions, and score reading reasoning separately from spelling.
